{
  "id": "80910",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "The Autoionization of Water",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We have seen that water can function as either an acid or a base, depending on the nature of the solute dissolved in it. In fact, in pure water or in any aqueous solution, water acts both as an acid and a base. A very small fraction of water molecules donates protons to other water molecules to form hydronium ions and hydroxide ions:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4xeiggapqc9cg6v",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Lewis structures for the autoionization of water",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "bwxdto05r2rqfkn",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fiwdnyhpa387a86",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/32/323d651799851be0fac997d8ae6e51d9/autoionization.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "y6zh6k2v0wzts5i",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "64a7l81xnx0ppik",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\require{color}\\underset{\\color[rgb]{1.0, 0.0, 0.0}\\textbf{acid}}{{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O}\\;+\\;\\underset{\\color[rgb]{0.0, 0.0, 1.0}\\textbf{base}}{{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O}\\;\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\underset{\\color[rgb]{1.0, 0.0, 0.0}\\textbf{acid}}{{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+}\\;+\\underset{\\color[rgb]{0.0, 0.0, 1.0}\\textbf{base}}{\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-}\\;\\;",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This type of reaction, in which a substance ionizes when one molecule of the substance reacts with another molecule of the same substance, is referred to as "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "autoionization"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The process in which water spontaneously produces low concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions through the transfer of protons between water molecules."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1955938902",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3146499928",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "sj6671ftzch4tqc",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Pure water undergoes autoionization to a very slight extent. Only about two out of every 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "9"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecules in a sample of pure water are ionized at 25 °C. The equilibrium constant for the ionization of water is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "ion-product constant for water ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "w"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The equilibrium constant for the autoionization of water."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "799285705",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "183045906",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ":"
              }
            ],
            "id": "oy057y01s3nphgq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "mxxtpzugru1r3hg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "k4v0ro160m7twt2",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq) \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathit\\;K_{\\mathrm w}\\;=\\;\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{OH}^-\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Using H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " as a shorthand way to represent the hydrated proton, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": ", we can also write this as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "h0m5u4gcv5alncd",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rhc3762fcql474n",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m954pmub0amdxvq",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm H^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; K_{\\mathrm w}\\;=\\;\\lbrack\\mathrm H^+\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{OH}^-\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The slight ionization of pure water is reflected in the small value of the equilibrium constant; at 25 °C, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "w"
              },
              {
                "text": " has a value of 1.0 × 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−14"
              },
              {
                "text": ". This process is endothermic, so the extent of ionization and the resulting concentrations of hydronium ion and hydroxide ion increase with temperature. For example, at 100 °C, the value for "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "w"
              },
              {
                "text": " is about 5.6 × 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−13"
              },
              {
                "text": ", roughly 50 times larger than the value at 25 °C."
              }
            ],
            "id": "6qsjnnrny2e2xpp",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "690225106",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
